   251	**3** **STUDY DEFINITION AND PLANNING**
   252	
   253	
   254	The _object_ of this study are Pythonic functional constructs, and,
   255	specifically, lambdas, comprehensions, and MRF. The _quality focus_
   256	refers to their understandability, especially during a change task.
   257	The _perspective_ is of researchers interested in distilling guidelines
   258	for developers, educators, and researchers/tool builders to facilitate
   259	the comprehension and maintenance of Python code. The _context_
   260	consists of 209 paid developers recruited through the Prolific platform, and code snippets written using functional constructs, as
   261	well as their procedural alternatives. The study aims to address the
   262	following three research questions:
   263	(1) **RQ** 1 **:** _How difficult is it to modify code snippets relying on_
   264	_functional constructs compared to their procedural alterna-_
   265	_tives?_ This research question aims to assess, through change
   266	tasks, the understandability of code written using functional
   267	constructs versus their procedural alternatives.
   268	(2) **RQ** 2 **:** _To what extent do functional constructs influence the_
   269	_perceived source code understandability?_ Here we assess the
   270	perceived understandability of a code snippet using a functional construct versus its procedural alternative. This is
   271	achieved by showing two functionally equivalent code snippets written with and without functional constructs.
   272	(3) **RQ** 3 **:** _Why do developers use functional constructs, and in_
   273	_which scenarios?_ The last research question qualitatively
   274	investigates the reasons why and when developers use functional constructs, but also the perceived disadvantages.
   275	
   276	
   277	**3.1** **Context Selection - Study Participants**
   278	
   279	
   280	The study was conducted by leveraging 209 paid developers recruited using the Prolific [ 15 ] platform. As highlighted by Tahaei
   281	and Vaniea [ 40 ], this is the only platform that could involve an adequate number of participants who pass the programming questions.
   282	Note that Prolific has been previously used in software engineering
   283	research on defect prediction [ 17 ], or tool evaluation [ 32 ]. Existing
   284	studies [ 7, 33, 35 ] highlighted the “vague meaning of programming
   285	experience”. Therefore, we have complemented a set of constraints
   286	on the Prolific platform, _e.g.,_ in terms of education, skills, and working experience to recruit developers, with further filters, _e.g.,_ filtering out all respondents not able to complete any of the six change
   287	tasks. Specifically, we have selected the participants on several
   288	
   289	
   290	A Study on the Pythonic Functional Constructs’ Understandability ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal
   291	
   292	
   293	
   294	requirements: (i) the subject of study was computer science, (ii)
   295	they declared to know computer programming, (iii) Python was
   296	listed as one of the known programming languages, and (iv) for
   297	the education level, we limited our choices to technical/community
   298	college, undergraduate ( _e.g.,_ bachelor), graduate ( _e.g.,_ master), or
   299	doctorate degree.
   300	Given that we estimated a task duration between 40 minutes and
   301	
   302	1 hour, and considering our available budget, we offered a payment
   303	of 12 UK Pounds-hour (the minimum as per Prolific’s policy is 8
   304	UK Pounds-hour).
   305	
   306	
   307	**3.2** **Context Selection - Study Objects**
   308	
   309	
   310	Since our experiment shows code snippets to developers and asks
   311	them to perform certain tasks, we need to properly select representative ones. To avoid artificial code snippets, we started identifying
   312	random instances of the investigated functional constructs from
   313	an existing dataset [ 44 ]. The selection was made to select, for each
   314	type of functional construct, a set of code elements with varying
   315	complexity. The complexity is computed as McCabe’s cyclomatic
   316	complexity [ 24 ], by adding one to the number of conditionals, iterations, and generators.
   317	In particular, we picked: 10 lambda usages with a complexity
   318	between 1 and 4; 10 comprehension usages, with a complexity
   319	between 2 and 6; and 3 map, 3 reduce, and 3 filter usages.
   320	After the selection, we isolated each code snippet from its surrounding context, by creating constant initialization for the used
   321	variables. Then, for each code example, one author produced the
   322	procedural alternative, that has been double-checked by a different
   323	author, both having at least three years of Python programming experience. Specifically, we replaced lambda with an explicit function
   324	definition, while comprehensions have been replaced by nested
   325	loops and conditionals. For MRF, we replaced their invocation with
   326	the explicit implementation of the required operation, _e.g.,_ iterating
   327	through all the elements of a list and applying to all of them the
   328	desired operation. As a last step, we made sure that each code snippet produced an output so that it was possible to objectively assess
   329	its outcome, as well as we verified that, given the same input, the
   330	output of the functional code snippet was perfectly similar to the
   331	one obtained with the “hand-crafted” procedural alternative.
   332	
   333	
   334	**3.3** **Dependent and independent variables**
   335	
   336	
   337	The study _dependent variable_ is the code understandability, measured as change task correctness for RQ 1, and perceived understandability for RQ 2 . The _independent variable_ is the use of a functional
   338	construct, as opposed to its procedural alternative. While the analysis has been done on multiple constructs (lambdas, comprehensions,
   339	and MRF), this is not a variable of the study, as we do not directly
   340	compare constructs with each other. In addition, we account for
   341	_co-factors_ related to constructs’ complexity and developers’ characteristics. In the following section, we further detail how variables
   342	are measured, and what co-factors are.
   343	
   344	
   345	**3.4** **Experiment Design and Planning**
   346	
   347	
   348	The experiment consists of a series of questions, asked to each
   349	participant through a Web-based questionnaire (a Google form).
   350	The questionnaire was organized into 4 sections.
